<header><p class="st-kicker">nomadic confederation · Central Asia and the Steppe</p><h1 class="st-title">Torks</h1><p class="st-meta">985 CE – 1065 CE (approx.)</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Oghuz Turkic nomads of the southern Russian steppe who fought the Rus' and later served them as frontier guards.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>The Torks were <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oghuz-turks">Oghuz Turks</a> who moved west from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aral-sea">Aral</a> and Caspian steppes in the late 10th century, part of the westward migrations that followed the collapse of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/oghuz-yabgu-state">Oghuz Yabgu State</a>. Byzantine writers called them Uzes. In 985 they appear as allies of Prince <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vladimir-the-great">Vladimir of Kiev</a> in his campaign against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/volga">Volga</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bulgars">Bulgars</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The Torks were pastoral nomads organised in clans under chiefs, with no strong central power. They competed with the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/pechenegs">Pechenegs</a> for the steppe north of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/black-sea">Black Sea</a> and raided both Rus' and the Byzantine <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/balkans">Balkans</a>; a large Uz horde crossed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/danube">Danube</a> in 1064 before being destroyed by disease and Bulgarian attacks.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>In 1060 a combined Rus' army defeated the Torks, and the arrival of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cumans">Cumans</a> soon after forced them from the steppe. Many settled as allies of the Kievan princes along the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ros-river">Ros River</a>, among the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chorni-klobuky">Chorni Klobuky</a>, 'Black Hats', who guarded the frontier.</p><p class="st-conf">Few sources survive for this state; details are uncertain.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><div class="st-emblem" role="img" aria-label="nomadic confederation, 985 CE – 1065 CE (approx.)"><svg viewBox="0 0 100 100" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M18 74 V58 Q50 30 82 58 V74 Z"/><path d="M42 74 V60 H58 V74"/><path d="M30 50 L50 36 L70 50"/><path d="M50 36 V26"/></svg><p class="st-emblem-type">nomadic confederation</p><p class="st-emblem-region">Central Asia and the Steppe</p><div class="st-span" aria-hidden="true"><span class="st-span-bar" style="left:79.30%;width:1.59%"></span></div><div class="st-span-axis" aria-hidden="true"><span>3000 BCE</span><span>1 CE</span><span>2000</span></div></div><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>985 CE – 1065 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>nomadic confederation</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>nomadic</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Tengrism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Oghuz Turkic</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd>Ukraine, Russia</dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Uzes, Oghuz (Rus' steppe)</dd></dl></aside></div>
